# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `ThreadAbortException` are **not supported in .NET 8** — they throw `PlatformNotSupportedException`. You must replace this with cooperative cancellation.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource _cts;
    private readonly Channel<WorkItem> _channel;

    public BackgroundProcessor()
    {
        // Bounded channel provides backpressure; unbounded if you prefer
        _channel = Channel.CreateBounded<WorkItem>(capacity: 100);
    }

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
        {
            try
            {
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        timeout ??= TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2);
        _channel.Writer.Complete(); // signal no more items

        // Give the loop time to drain gracefully
        var completed = _workerTask.WaitAsync(timeout.Value);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(completed, Task.Delay(timeout.Value)) != completed)
        {
            // Graceful drain failed — cancel forcefully
            _cts.Cancel();
        }

        await _workerTask; // observe exceptions
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| .NET Framework Pattern | .NET 8 Replacement | Pitfall |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` if called |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | *(removed)* | No equivalent needed — no abort to reset |
| `ThreadAbortException` | `OperationCanceledException` | Catch the new exception type |
| Blocking `GetNextItem()` | `Channel<T>.ReadAllAsync()` | Blocking calls ignore cancellation tokens — use async APIs |
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationToken` | Token integrates with async/await; volatile bool doesn't unblock waits |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `Task.WhenAny` + timeout | Sleep blocks the calling thread and provides no feedback |

## Migration Checklist

1. **Replace `Thread` with `Task.Run`** — background threads map to tasks.
2. **Replace `Thread.Abort()` with `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — this is the only cooperative stop mechanism in .NET 8.
3. **Replace blocking collections with `Channel<T>`** — channels support `CancellationToken` natively and won't deadlock on shutdown.
4. **Pass `CancellationToken` through the entire call chain** — any `GetNextItem()` that blocks without a token will hang on shutdown.
5. **Implement `IDisposable`** — clean up the `CancellationTokenSource`.
6. **Make `Stop` async** — allows callers to await graceful shutdown without `Thread.Sleep` hacks.

## If You Can't Make Everything Async

For legacy code where `ProcessItem` is synchronous and can't accept a token:

```csharp
private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
{
    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        try
        {
            // Poll with timeout instead of indefinite blocking
            if (_channel.Reader.TryRead(out var item))
                ProcessItem(item);
            else
                token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(100));
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            RollbackCurrentTransaction();
            break;
        }
        finally
        {
            FlushBuffers();
        }
    }
}
```

The key insight: .NET 8 has **no mechanism to forcibly terminate a thread mid-execution**. Your code *must* check the cancellation token at regular intervals or use async APIs that accept one.
